<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Portmahomack</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Portmahomack is a village on the Tarbat peninsula in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-ross">Easter Ross</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. Excavations from 1994 to 2007 revealed a Pictish monastery of the 6th to 9th centuries beneath and around its church, with workshops for vellum, metalwork and glass and fragments of fine carved stones. The monastery was burnt around 800, probably in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raid. It is the best-known Pictish monastic site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/portmahomack.jpg" alt="Portmahomack" width="320" height="231" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ordnance Survey · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tarbat_ness_lighthouse_OS_map_25inch_Rosshire_XXX.7_1880.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Scottish Gaelic name, Port Mo Chalmaig, means the haven of Saint Colmóc. The village lies on a sandy bay on the northern coast of the Tarbat Peninsula, in the parish of Tarbat, about nine miles east of Tain. Its small harbour was designed by Thomas Telford, and the village is unusual in lying on the east coast while facing west. Tarbat Ness Lighthouse stands about three miles away at the end of the peninsula.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Shell middens point to settlement as early as one or two thousand years BCE, and the remains of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> broch lie a little to the west of the village. In 1949 the archaeologist O. G. S. Crawford speculated that a square fortification near Port a' Chaistell, described by a minister in 1822, might have been a Roman camp, though no trace of Roman settlement was later found there.</p><p>The excavations directed by Martin Carver around the redundant St Colman's church found an enclosure ditch, a burial ground with cist and head-support burials, the remains of a stone church and at least four monumental stone crosses. Over two hundred pieces of sculpture were recovered. The scholar Victoria Whitworth has suggested that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-kells">Book of Kells</a>, whose display capitals resemble the sculptures, may derive from this monastery rather than from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a>.</p><p>Later conflicts touched the peninsula. The Battle of Tarbat Ness was fought around 1030 to 1040 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thorfinn-the-mighty">Thorfinn the Mighty</a>, Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caithness">Caithness</a>, and the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, and in the 1480s men of the Clan Ross cornered a raiding party of the Clan Mackay in the Tarbat church and set it on fire. Cod, ling, halibut and other fish were taken in great quantities from the 17th century, and herring exports peaked between 1850 and 1890 before steam trawlers brought decline; grain exports from the harbour ended in the 1930s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The village has a permanent population of between 500 and 600. The former parish church, whose oldest part may date from the 9th century, houses the Tarbat Discovery Centre with finds from the excavations, while some of the carved stones are shown in the Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>. Two storehouses by the harbour, built in the late 17th century and in 1779, survive as housing.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Portmahomack” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Christianity spread among the Picts between the 5th and 7th centuries, through missions from Iona and contacts with Northumbria, and monasteries such as <b>Portmahomack</b> on the Tarbat peninsula, where vellum was prepared for books, became centres of learning and craft.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-picts">Kingdom of the Picts · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Easter Ross later became an important district of the Kingdom of the Picts, with major monastic and sculptural sites such as <b>Portmahomack</b>, Nigg and Shandwick.</blockquote><a href="/state/decantae">Decantae</a></li><li><blockquote>Major centres included the fortress of Burghead and the monastery of <b>Portmahomack</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-fortriu">Kingdom of Fortriu</a></li></ul></section>
